---
/**
 * About.
 *
 * The one page where the frameworks may be named directly. Everywhere else
 * they have to show up as consequences inside a decision — a framework named
 * without a consequence reads as coursework, which is the opposite of the
 * point. No grades anywhere: they inform which projects lead, they are not
 * content.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { projectHref } from '../data/projects';
---

<Base
    title="About — Tomme Denney"
    description="Spatial and immersive product designer who prototypes. MS Extended Reality with a concentration in Human-Computer Interaction, BFA Game Design — Northeastern University."
    path="/about"
>
    <SiteNav current="about" />

    <main id="main" class="shell">
        {/* This was the homepage masthead. It reads better here, where a
            reader has already decided they want to know who made the work. */}
        <header class="mast">
            <p class="label">Tomme Denney &middot; Boston, Massachusetts</p>
            <h1 class="display" transition:name="page-heading">
                I design experiences you <span class="mast-accent">enter</span>, not ones you look at.
            </h1>
            <div class="mast-body">
                <p class="lede">
                    Spatial and immersive product designer — and enough of a builder to
                    test an idea in a headset instead of arguing about it in a deck.
                </p>
                <div class="prose muted">
                    <p>
                        MS in Extended Reality with a concentration in Human-Computer
                        Interaction, and a BFA in Game Design, both from Northeastern
                        University. Several years building XR for real clients.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        I treat comfort as an accessibility problem rather than a polish
                        pass: locomotion, session length, play space and the person who
                        has never worn a headset are design questions from the first sketch.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </header>

        <section class="band">
            <div class="band-grid">
            <p class="label band-label">How I actually work</p>
            <div class="prose">
            <p>
                Five phases, run end to end on two graduate projects in
                <em>Design for Dignity</em>, with a graded deliverable at each one.
                I show this sequence only on projects that genuinely went through
                it — a process diagram on a project that skipped ideation is a lie
                with a nice icon.
            </p>
            <ol class="phases">
                <li><span>Research</span></li>
                <li><span>Definition</span></li>
                <li><span>Ideation</span></li>
                <li><span>Synthesis</span></li>
                <li><span>Delivery</span></li>
            </ol>
            </div></div>
        </section>

        <section class="band">
            <div class="band-grid">
            <p class="label band-label">What I mean by dignity</p>
            <div class="prose">
            <p>
                From graduate coursework in <em>Design for Dignity</em>: a design
                either honours four things about a person or erodes them.
                <strong>Merit</strong> — is this someone of inherent worth, or a
                funnel stage? <strong>Autonomy</strong> — can they choose, refuse,
                exit, and control how they are represented?
                <strong>Human rights</strong> — what are they entitled to
                regardless of whether they are a valuable user?
                <strong>Interpersonal connection</strong> — does this strengthen a
                relationship between people, or stand in for one?
            </p>
            <p>
                I would rather show it than claim it. In
                <a href={projectHref('Inhabiting Memory')}>Inhabiting Memory</a>,
                every interpretive room came from that person's own interview
                rather than my reading of them, and I ruled out the most obvious
                mechanic available — letting a visitor handle a deceased relative
                as an object — because it treats a person as a prop. Those are the
                pillars working as build constraints, which is the only form in
                which they mean anything.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="band">
            <div class="band-grid">
            <p class="label band-label">Instrumented evaluation</p>
            <div class="prose">
            <p>
                Graduate coursework in <em>Biometrics of Design</em> put me on eye
                tracking and heart-rate instrumentation — measuring where
                attention actually went and what the body did, rather than asking
                someone to recall it afterwards. It is the least common thing on
                this site and the one I would most like to use again: self-report
                tells you what a person believes about an experience, and
                instrumentation tells you what happened to them.
            </p>
            </div></div>
        </section>

        <section class="band">
            <div class="band-grid">
            <p class="label band-label">Where the rest of it comes from</p>
            <div class="prose">
            <p>
                Game interface design — diegetic UI, iconography, and taking a
                sketch through to a tested prototype. Interaction and experience
                foundations — object surveys, heuristic critique, and low-fidelity
                to high-fidelity wireframes. XR history and theory, which is where
                the argument that spatial media behaves differently from a screen
                actually comes from. And psychology, which is what any claim about
                attention, memory or cognitive load has to rest on.
            </p>
            </div></div>
        </section>

        <p class="band" style="padding-bottom:0"><a class="btn" href="/work">See the work &rarr;</a></p>
    </main>

    <SiteFooter />
</Base>
